To illustrate the typical variance of the sliding-window patches, Fig.  4  shows the predicted values of each cropped patch for  UTS  and  CVN  compared to the experimentally measured value for a randomly selected LOGO-CV fold. Despite the quite large variance for each specimen, the average for each training specimen accurately reflects the measured value. One possible explanation for the large variance may be that the selected patch size is too small to accurately represent all physically required information. If a patch at 1000x magnification only contains pearlite, the model should predict a higher value for  UTS  and a lower value when only ferrite is visible in the patch. However, this behavior is very similar across all magnifications, even when each single patch contains multiple grains. Figure 4 Example parity plots for  UTS  and  CVN  for 25x magnification visualize the model’s prediction variance for each steel specimen. Each small dot reflects the prediction of a 384x384 px patch from the original microstructure image. The  X  marks the average prediction for each micrograph and the large dot ( ∙ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
