nted by micrographs alone. Additionally, the values indicate that for all metrics and properties higher magnification generally results in higher errors. This becomes especially clear, when normalizing the MAE to get all errors on the same scale, as shown in Table  4 . Then, even training data shows a higher error with higher magnification (going from 0.08 to 0.37 NMAE).

A fair comparison of metrics to previous work is difficult, as often the final validation metrics are calculated using only small amount of specimens or are likely containing data leakage (RQ2 and cf. Section  3.3 , in which we show how simply changing the data splitting strategy can drastically influence the prediction results). The most related study, which uses a similar data splitting approach as we do, reaches a similar level of MAE (32.2 HB) when predicting the Brinell Hardness in the range from 300 to 700 HB based on the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) microstructures of 52 specimens [ 31 ]. According to Matyunin et al. [ 62 ] the Brinell Hardness can be compared well to the  UTS  as a proportional relation between both can be established for many materials, including some types of steel. We estimate the MAPE of their result to be between  32.2 HB / 700 HB × 100 = 4.6 % \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
